      "a": "Given Data:\nSpeed of bullet hitting the ice\n$ = {\\text{V}} = 840\\;{\\text{m/s}}$\nHeat produced due to kinetic energy of the bullet: -\n$ = \\dfrac{1}{2}m{V^2}$\nNow,\n${\\text{m}} = $\nMass of bullet\n$ = 50\\;{\\text{g}} = \\left( {50 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right){\\text{Kg}}$\nHence\n$ \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2}m{V^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\left( {50 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right) \\times {(840)^2}$\n$ = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 50 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 705600$\n$ = 17640\\;{\\text{J}}$\n$ = \\dfrac{{17640}}{{4.2}}{\\text{Cal}}[\\because $\nTo convert\n${\\text{J}} \\to {\\text{Cal}}$\nwe divide by 4.2]\n$\\left. {\\dfrac{1}{2}m{V^2} = 4200{\\text{Cal}} \\to 1} \\right)$\nNow, heat given by bullet due to temperature difference\n$ = {\\text{m}}$\nc\n$\\left( {{Q_2} - {Q_1}} \\right)$\n$ = 50 \\times 0.02(30 - 0)$\n$ = 30{\\text{Cal}} \\to 2$\n)\nFrom 1) & 2)\n${\\text{m}}$\n= Mass of bullet\n${\\text{c}} = $\nSpecific heat of bullet\n${{\\text{Q}}_2} = $\nInitial Temperature\n${{\\text{Q}}_1} = $\nFinal Temperature\nTotal heat given by bullet\n$ = 4200 + 30 = 4230{\\text{Cal}}$\n.\nNow, entire heat of bullet is used in melting the ice only, let\n${\\text{M}} = $\nMass of Ice that melted\n${\\text{L}} = $\nLatent heat of ice\nHence\n$m \\times L = 4230$\n$m = \\dfrac{{4230}}{L} = \\dfrac{{4230}}{{80}}$\n$m = 52.88g$"
